1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:03,126
Remember our two-knob taxi-fare machine?

2
00:00:03,126 --> 00:00:04,831
It learned how fares change

3
00:00:04,831 --> 00:00:06,347
with distance just fine.

4
00:00:06,697 --> 00:00:09,281
But fares also depend on the time of day:

5
00:00:09,281 --> 00:00:10,542
rush hour costs more

6
00:00:10,542 --> 00:00:12,622
and late night a little more too.

7
00:00:12,972 --> 00:00:14,198
It can't learn that

8
00:00:14,198 --> 00:00:15,682
because all it can draw

9
00:00:15,682 --> 00:00:16,908
is a straight line.

10
00:00:17,258 --> 00:00:18,460
So how does a machine

11
00:00:18,460 --> 00:00:20,006
learn a pattern that bends?

12
00:00:20,006 --> 00:00:21,266
With a neural network.

13
00:00:26,967 --> 00:00:28,974
Last time, turning the knobs was like

14
00:00:28,974 --> 00:00:30,494
walking downhill blindfolded

15
00:00:30,494 --> 00:00:31,633
always stepping where

16
00:00:31,633 --> 00:00:32,935
the score drops fastest.

17
00:00:33,285 --> 00:00:34,884
Today we'll build a tiny

18
00:00:34,884 --> 00:00:36,750
network that learns the bend

19
00:00:36,750 --> 00:00:38,349
see why more layers help

20
00:00:38,349 --> 00:00:39,482
and meet AlexNet.

21
00:00:43,600 --> 00:00:45,464
Here's some made-up data:

22
00:00:45,464 --> 00:00:48,073
the same 5-kilometer ride, 48 times

23
00:00:48,073 --> 00:00:50,309
at different hours of the day.

24
00:00:50,659 --> 00:00:52,594
Daytime rides cost about $23

25
00:00:52,594 --> 00:00:55,288
just what our two-knob machine charges.

26
00:00:55,638 --> 00:00:56,593
Around 8 a.m.

27
00:00:56,593 --> 00:00:58,062
and 6 p.m. it spikes

28
00:00:58,062 --> 00:01:00,779
and late at night it's a bit pricier.

29
00:01:01,129 --> 00:01:02,102
Same two knobs

30
00:01:02,102 --> 00:01:04,326
one for height and one for slope

31
00:01:04,326 --> 00:01:06,202
but now we feed it the time

32
00:01:06,202 --> 00:01:07,384
not the distance.

33
00:01:07,734 --> 00:01:08,909
However you turn them

34
00:01:08,909 --> 00:01:10,475
the best you get is a nearly

35
00:01:10,475 --> 00:01:12,097
flat line through the middle.

36
00:01:12,447 --> 00:01:14,270
It undercharges at rush

37
00:01:14,270 --> 00:01:16,490
hour and overcharges by day.

38
00:01:16,490 --> 00:01:19,582
Its loss, the score for how wrong it is

39
00:01:19,582 --> 00:01:21,247
can't get below 11.5.

40
00:01:21,597 --> 00:01:23,665
The knobs aren't the problem:

41
00:01:23,665 --> 00:01:26,376
this machine only draws straight lines

42
00:01:26,376 --> 00:01:27,945
and the pattern bends.

43
00:01:32,067 --> 00:01:33,930
The fix isn't one big machine.

44
00:01:33,930 --> 00:01:35,917
It's lots of simple little ones.

45
00:01:36,267 --> 00:01:37,802
Each unit still has two

46
00:01:37,802 --> 00:01:39,271
knobs and draws a line

47
00:01:39,271 --> 00:01:40,205
plus one rule:

48
00:01:40,205 --> 00:01:42,408
anything below zero becomes zero.

49
00:01:42,758 --> 00:01:45,461
Its line stays flat, then bends upward.

50
00:01:45,811 --> 00:01:47,252
The two knobs set where

51
00:01:47,252 --> 00:01:49,195
it bends and how steep it gets.

52
00:01:49,545 --> 00:01:50,899
One bend isn't enough

53
00:01:50,899 --> 00:01:52,639
so add more units that bend

54
00:01:52,639 --> 00:01:53,864
in different spots.

55
00:01:54,214 --> 00:01:56,780
Add them up, and you can build bumps

56
00:01:56,780 --> 00:01:58,490
steps, almost any curve.

57
00:01:58,840 --> 00:02:00,849
Our network has 12 units side

58
00:02:00,849 --> 00:02:02,650
by side in the first layer

59
00:02:02,650 --> 00:02:04,521
and one unit in the second.

60
00:02:04,871 --> 00:02:06,322
That unit scales each

61
00:02:06,322 --> 00:02:07,773
bent line with a knob

62
00:02:07,773 --> 00:02:08,602
adds them up

63
00:02:08,602 --> 00:02:11,089
then one last knob shifts the total.

64
00:02:11,089 --> 00:02:12,194
That's the fare.

65
00:02:12,544 --> 00:02:14,293
Thirty-seven knobs in all.

66
00:02:14,643 --> 00:02:16,240
Layers of little units like

67
00:02:16,240 --> 00:02:18,548
this are what we call a neural network.

68
00:02:18,898 --> 00:02:21,267
The name comes from neurons in the brain

69
00:02:21,267 --> 00:02:23,281
but don't take that too seriously.

70
00:02:24,800 --> 00:02:26,419
To turn all thirty-seven

71
00:02:26,419 --> 00:02:28,241
we use last episode's trick

72
00:02:28,241 --> 00:02:30,130
nudging every knob whichever

73
00:02:30,130 --> 00:02:31,951
way drops the loss fastest.

74
00:02:32,301 --> 00:02:34,829
Our curve starts as a random squiggle

75
00:02:34,829 --> 00:02:36,537
and step by step it bends

76
00:02:36,537 --> 00:02:37,699
toward the peaks.

77
00:02:38,049 --> 00:02:39,685
After 10,000 steps

78
00:02:39,685 --> 00:02:41,958
the loss is down to 0.26.

79
00:02:41,958 --> 00:02:43,958
The best straight line

80
00:02:43,958 --> 00:02:45,867
never got below 11.5.

81
00:02:46,217 --> 00:02:47,946
Rush hours and late nights

82
00:02:47,946 --> 00:02:49,276
it catches them all.

83
00:02:49,626 --> 00:02:51,401
And every corner on this curve

84
00:02:51,401 --> 00:02:53,472
is one little unit making its bend.

85
00:02:53,822 --> 00:02:54,369
By the way

86
00:02:54,369 --> 00:02:56,504
there's a much faster trick for finding

87
00:02:56,504 --> 00:02:58,037
which way to turn each knob.

88
00:02:58,037 --> 00:02:59,296
More on that next time.

89
00:03:03,400 --> 00:03:06,101
So far, we've put units side by side

90
00:03:06,101 --> 00:03:07,677
each adding one bend.

91
00:03:08,027 --> 00:03:08,917
Now stack them

92
00:03:08,917 --> 00:03:10,697
so each layer folds the line

93
00:03:10,697 --> 00:03:12,540
it gets from the layer before

94
00:03:12,540 --> 00:03:13,748
like folding paper.

95
00:03:14,098 --> 00:03:16,781
For example, two units per layer can fold

96
00:03:16,781 --> 00:03:18,809
a slanted line exactly in half.

97
00:03:19,159 --> 00:03:21,222
One layer makes two straight pieces

98
00:03:21,222 --> 00:03:22,400
two layers make four

99
00:03:22,400 --> 00:03:23,815
and in this example each

100
00:03:23,815 --> 00:03:25,170
new layer doubles that.

101
00:03:25,520 --> 00:03:27,968
So ten units side by side

102
00:03:27,968 --> 00:03:30,221
give at most 11 pieces.

103
00:03:30,221 --> 00:03:33,746
Stacked five deep, they can give 32.

104
00:03:34,096 --> 00:03:35,573
Later layers reuse what

105
00:03:35,573 --> 00:03:37,372
earlier ones already folded.

106
00:03:37,722 --> 00:03:38,902
The deeper the stack

107
00:03:38,902 --> 00:03:40,494
the more complex the shapes

108
00:03:40,494 --> 00:03:41,969
the same units can build.

109
00:03:42,319 --> 00:03:44,120
That's the "deep" in deep learning.

110
00:03:48,233 --> 00:03:51,089
In 2012, three researchers at the University

111
00:03:51,089 --> 00:03:54,074
of Toronto trained a very deep neural network.

112
00:03:54,424 --> 00:03:57,081
Their network became known as AlexNet.

113
00:03:57,431 --> 00:04:00,117
It had 8 layers and 60 million knobs.

114
00:04:00,117 --> 00:04:02,078
It learned 1,000 categories

115
00:04:02,078 --> 00:04:03,820
from 1.2 million photos.

116
00:04:04,170 --> 00:04:06,107
At that year's ImageNet contest

117
00:04:06,107 --> 00:04:08,045
it got five guesses per picture

118
00:04:08,045 --> 00:04:10,482
and only missed if all five were wrong.

119
00:04:10,832 --> 00:04:14,348
The version they entered got 15.3% wrong.

120
00:04:14,348 --> 00:04:16,406
The runner-up got 26.2%.

121
00:04:16,756 --> 00:04:18,476
Nobody told it what to look for

122
00:04:18,476 --> 00:04:19,974
yet its first layer learned

123
00:04:19,974 --> 00:04:21,639
to spot stripes at every angle

124
00:04:21,639 --> 00:04:22,804
and patches of color.

125
00:04:23,154 --> 00:04:24,888
Later layers combined those into

126
00:04:24,888 --> 00:04:26,567
more and more complex patterns.

127
00:04:26,917 --> 00:04:28,779
Removing any middle layer made

128
00:04:28,779 --> 00:04:30,579
its first-guess accuracy drop

129
00:04:30,579 --> 00:04:32,193
about 2 percentage points.

130
00:04:32,543 --> 00:04:33,707
As the paper put it

131
00:04:33,707 --> 00:04:35,668
"the depth really is important."

132
00:04:37,200 --> 00:04:38,944
So a neural network is just

133
00:04:38,944 --> 00:04:40,882
simple little units with knobs

134
00:04:40,882 --> 00:04:42,368
stacked layer on layer.

135
00:04:42,718 --> 00:04:43,963
Stack them deep enough

136
00:04:43,963 --> 00:04:45,208
and they can recognize

137
00:04:45,208 --> 00:04:46,679
really complicated things.

138
00:04:47,029 --> 00:04:49,905
Thirty-seven knobs, we can test one by one.

139
00:04:49,905 --> 00:04:51,108
But sixty million?

140
00:04:51,108 --> 00:04:52,780
How do you find which way

141
00:04:52,780 --> 00:04:53,917
to turn them all?

142
00:04:54,267 --> 00:04:56,765
That's backpropagation, coming up next time.
